      "claim": "S(+)-apomorphine, a related aporphine, had a brain/plasma AUC ratio of 228% in mice, demonstrating robust CNS penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "N-propylnorapomorphine demonstrated good brain penetration ex vivo in rats and in vivo by PET in nonhuman primates, supporting CNS access for this alkaloid class.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "Blonanserin had a human brain/plasma concentration ratio of 3.38 by PET, indicating good BBB permeability and supporting meaningful CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Multiple high-transfer analogues, especially aporphine and related isoquinoline alkaloids, show direct brain uptake, quantitative brain/plasma ratios, or PET-verified CNS penetration; the query noscapine shares the N-methyltetrahydroisoquinoline and dimethoxyaryl pharmacophore, supporting meaningful CNS access.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct noscapine-specific quantitative brain/plasma or CSF measurement is present in the retrieved evidence; reliance is on high-transfer analogues.",
    "Some supportive records are qualitative tissue detection and do not quantify unbound brain exposure."
  ],
